thread:
rachel: Heads-up: Android SDK users reporting intermittent 429s followed by huge latency spikes. Repro on Pixel 4 emu API 31 when running many parallel requests from app. Suspect our retry/backoff + okhttp dispatcher interaction.

devon: Do you have a minimal repro? 429s imply server throttling, but retries could amplify. Which backoff are we doing in the SDK?

rachel: Default is exponential with capped jitter implemented as an Interceptor that retries on 429/503 up to 3 times. Code: 

rachel: ```java
for (int i=0;i<3;i++) {
  Response res = chain.proceed(chain.request());
  if (res.code() == 429 || res.code() == 503) {
     Thread.sleep((1 << i) * 100L + jitter());
     continue;
  }
  return res;
}
return lastResponse;
```

 taylor: Problem: blocking Thread.sleep in an interceptor will block okhttp's dispatcher thread. If dispatcher threads are occupied sleeping, new calls will queue. That plus retryOnConnectionFailure true can cause compounded retries.

devon: Oof. We shouldn't block the dispatcher. Use asynchronous retry scheduling or implement backoff in caller logic. Also consider setting Dispatcher.maxRequests lower and maxRequestsPerHost to avoid stampeding.

rachel: We also had a sample that recommended keeping retry logic inside an Interceptor for simplicity. Should we change that?

taylor: Yes — change sample to use non-blocking backoff. On Android use Handler/Looper postDelayed or schedule on a separate single-threaded executor. Example: enqueue a new call after delay instead of sleeping in interceptor.

devon: Another gotcha: if interceptor reissues calls synchronously it can re-enter the same interceptor chain leading to resource buildup. Use a RetryToken header to mark a retried attempt and avoid infinite loops.

infra-bot: Note created issue DX-221 to replace blocking retries in SDK samples with async retries and document server-side throttling guidance.

rachel: Quick test: swapped interceptor to immediate fail on 429 and let caller handle backoff with coroutine delay + exponential jitter. Smoke test with 200 concurrent client tasks normalised latency and no dispatcher saturation.

android-ci: CI run 2026-03-22T11:30:00Z: android-dx-smoke started.

taylor: Action items: (1) Update quickstart sample to show coroutine-based retry with delay and cancellation, (2) change SDK shipped interceptor to not block dispatcher (or remove it), (3) add note about retryOnConnectionFailure and streaming workloads.

rachel: I'll open PR for samples and add a short note in README. Assigning DX-221 to me.

devon: Also test on older Androids; Thread.sleep in interceptors had different effects on older OkHttp versions. Recommend bumping okhttp and adding a compatibility note.

rachel: Thanks all. Closing when PR merges.
